WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.091
A long list of numbers is hard to read.

2
00:00:04.091 --> 00:00:05.554
Groups are much easier:

3
00:00:05.554 --> 00:00:08.114
small, medium, and large families, for example.

4
00:00:08.114 --> 00:00:13.234
In this lesson, you will learn to group numbers into ranges with cut, and

5
00:00:13.234 --> 00:00:16.160
then count each group in a neat table.

6
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.720
Let's make our numbers tell a story.

7
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.189
On the right, the Viewer shows a frequency table of household size.

8
00:00:25.189 --> 00:00:27.866
Every single size has its own row:

9
00:00:27.866 --> 00:00:30.543
three, four, five, seven, and so on.

10
00:00:30.543 --> 00:00:33.220
It is long and hard to read.

11
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:35.079
I write a note:

12
00:00:35.079 --> 00:00:38.361
to make a range of column in a dataset.

13
00:00:38.361 --> 00:00:39.820
Then demographic, pipe, mutate.

14
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:50.931
Inside mutate:

15
00:00:50.931 --> 00:00:53.007
H H Size equals cut.

16
00:00:53.007 --> 00:00:57.160
Cut takes the number column, and cuts it into pieces.

17
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:05.884
First, breaks.

18
00:01:05.884 --> 00:01:09.018
The breaks are the edges of each group:

19
00:01:09.018 --> 00:01:11.760
one, five, ten, fifteen, twenty, and Inf.

20
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.262
Inf means infinity.

21
00:01:21.262 --> 00:01:26.684
It makes the last group open at the top, so every big family is

22
00:01:26.684 --> 00:01:28.620
included, no matter how large.

23
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.033
Then labels, one for each group, in quotes:

24
00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:43.900
one to five, five to ten, ten to fifteen, fifteen to twenty, and more than twenty.

25
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.773
Be careful with the brackets.

26
00:02:06.773 --> 00:02:10.120
There are several levels here, so close each one, slowly.

27
00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.313
Run it, and open the table.

28
00:03:02.313 --> 00:03:06.740
The household size column now shows the group names instead of numbers.

29
00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:16.771
Now let's count them.

30
00:03:16.771 --> 00:03:22.620
Cross underscore cases, from the expss package, with cell vars equals H H Size.

31
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.409
And here is our new table.

32
00:03:34.409 --> 00:03:41.720
Twenty four households have one to five members, and fifty seven have more than twenty. Much clearer! Wonderful work.

